WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:05.050
Scheduled posts used to sit in
a list in a key-value store.

2
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.691
A cron job woke every minute to
sweep it and run whatever was due.

3
00:00:10.091 --> 00:00:13.863
Cloudflare has a better way
to wait: a Durable Object,

4
00:00:13.863 --> 00:00:17.355
a small program with its
own storage and an alarm.

5
00:00:17.655 --> 00:00:22.456
The catch is that each Durable Object
can hold only one alarm at a time.

6
00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:27.504
So one scheduler object can't wait for
three posts at three different times.

7
00:00:28.004 --> 00:00:31.867
I rebuilt the scheduler to create
a new object for every task.

8
00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:37.077
You ask it something like: post
to Instagram at 9am tomorrow.

9
00:00:37.327 --> 00:00:41.658
A language model splits that into
which agent, what to do, and when.

10
00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:46.594
The scheduler checks the time is in
the future and makes a fresh task ID.

11
00:00:46.844 --> 00:00:52.557
It creates a Durable Object named after
that ID, stores the task inside it,

12
00:00:52.557 --> 00:00:54.309
and sets its one alarm.

13
00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:58.276
Three tasks means three objects,
each with its own clock.

14
00:00:58.676 --> 00:01:03.691
When an alarm rings, its object wakes
the right agent and hands it the job.

15
00:01:03.941 --> 00:01:05.499
Then it deletes the task.

16
00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:10.102
When I tested it, posts scheduled
through the API fired on time.

17
00:01:10.402 --> 00:01:14.857
From chat, some didn't, so the next
job was adding checks to that path.
